From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] vdev_file: keep file vdevs open across unmounts

The macOS layer closes all of a pool's file vdevs whenever any dataset of
the pool is unmounted (CLOSE_ON_UNMOUNT, vdev_file_close_on_unmount = 1),
so that file vdevs don't keep the host's volume busy at shutdown, and
reopens them on the next I/O. FreeBSD and Linux hold them open for the
pool's life, and the OpenZFS test suite relies on that:
zpool_import_missing_001_pos unmounts a dataset, removes a vdev's file
and expects the pool to keep working; on NeoDarwin the pool suspended,
the test hung and the rest of the zpool_import group failed behind it
(P3-01 checkpoint 2). The tunable isn't reachable as a sysctl here, so it
defaults to off. Shutdown with pools imported is root on ZFS's question
(P3-03).

Rebase-risk: low
---
diff --git a/module/os/neodarwin/zfs/vdev_file_os.c b/module/os/neodarwin/zfs/vdev_file_os.c
--- a/module/os/neodarwin/zfs/vdev_file_os.c
+++ b/module/os/neodarwin/zfs/vdev_file_os.c
@@ -73,7 +73,17 @@
  * kernel.
  *
  */
+#ifdef __NEODARWIN__
+/*
+ * Off: a pool keeps its file vdevs open, as on FreeBSD and Linux. On macOS
+ * they are closed whenever one of the pool's datasets is unmounted, so they
+ * don't keep the host volume busy at shutdown; a vdev file removed after
+ * that unmount then suspends the pool (zpool_import_missing_001_pos).
+ */
+static uint32_t vdev_file_close_on_unmount = 0;
+#else
 static uint32_t vdev_file_close_on_unmount = 1;
+#endif
 ZFS_MODULE_PARAM(zfs_vdev_file, vdev_file_, close_on_unmount, UINT, ZMOD_RW,
 	"close vdevs on unmount to avoid reboot hang");
 
